# Lesson Classes

<!-- Mechanism file, copied to AXMEM_MEMORY_DIR/classes.md by `axmem init` if
     one doesn't exist yet. Turns "is this the same kind of mistake as
     before?" from a mid-session guess into a machine check.
     Rules (enforced by manifest.cjs --check, invariant B6):
       1. Every live lesson may carry at most one `Class: [[class:<id>]]`
          line. From AXMEM_CLASS_REQUIRED_FROM on (default 2026-09-15 — a
          fresh install should set this to its own install date via env),
          a new lesson with no Class line is BLOCKED at write time; older
          unclassified lessons are only reported, never blocked.
       2. A class hub is an ordinary entry in THIS file: a header (the date
          matters — every parser here only recognises a `**20...**`
          header), one criterion sentence, and optionally the trigger(s)
          this class shares (a file-path anchor). The push channel matches
          a hub against an edit exactly like it matches any other entry:
          hit a hub's trigger -> it pushes the hub plus its newest live
          members; hit a member's own trigger -> it reports "same class N
          more" instead of spending an extra recall slot.
       3. No class fits? Add one in the SAME change (title + one criterion
          sentence). Classes are a controlled vocabulary, not a free-form
          tag — don't force a lesson into the nearest-but-wrong class.
       4. A class answers "what KIND of mistake was this", never "which
          project" (that's the repo's own LESSONS.md) or "how severe"
          (that's the canary).
     Query: grep -B3 'Class: \[\[class:<id>\]\]' AXMEM_MEMORY_DIR/lessons.md
     lists every member of a class.

     Example trigger syntax (this line is prose, not a live trigger — a real
     one is a `<!-- trigger: ... -->` comment INSIDE a class entry below,
     exactly like the ones lessons.md/decisions.md use):
       tool=Edit|Write; repo=<your-repo-id>; path=<repo-relative-glob-or-prefix*>
-->

## Entries

**2026-09-14 — Class: tool-channel byte rewriting** [class:tooling-escaping]
Criterion: the error comes from a shell/tool channel silently rewriting
bytes or meaning — halved backslashes, escape sequences, here-strings,
multi-word quoting, a pipe swallowing an exit code, CRLF, non-ASCII. The
symptom is "the code looks right, but what landed on disk / ran isn't what
was written." Fix: get the value from a real producer and read the bytes
back to verify, never hand-type the escaped form.

**2026-09-14 — Class: guard design is itself a defect surface** [class:guard-design]
Criterion: the error is IN the guard/gate/check layer — a guardrail that
never failed yet, a new gate introducing the same hole it closes elsewhere,
a judgment-style check that should have been a bounded count, "report only"
that quietly never became a gate, a self-test that can't prove red AND
green.

**2026-09-14 — Class: trust the instrument before the verdict** [class:instrument-validity]
Criterion: a number or a "passed" came from a measurement or verification
tool whose own correctness was never established — three lines failing at
once suggests the instrument, not the subject; a confound needs leveling
and a rerun before a conclusion; bundled changes can reverse a measured
direction. Prove the instrument first, then trust the causal story.

**2026-09-14 — Class: who reviews, how, and what counts as reviewed** [class:review-discipline]
Criterion: the error is in the review process itself — a prior conclusion
treated as evidence, a reviewer who never ran the primary path, a
same-model blind spot (builder = reviewer), a finding accepted without
reproducing it against the real code first.

**2026-09-14 — Class: shared state needs an explicit owner** [class:concurrency-and-sharing]
Criterion: the error comes from two writers touching the same resource
without anyone asking "who else writes this?" — a shared database, a shared
working tree, a rewrite that silently absorbs a concurrent write, a counter
that assumes exclusive access it doesn't have.

**2026-09-14 — Class: "works here" is not "works in the target environment"** [class:environment-parity]
Criterion: the error comes from treating two environments as one — a local
simulation that isn't faithful to CI/production, a config or dependency
version that differs by environment, a test that passed against a mock but
never against the real path.
